{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_Edilczhzqd0VYboH5pd9NZbD","content":"535\texplicit roots connecting them. Reclaim only unreachable implementation\n536\tstorage, not stream history or persistent datoms. Avoid handle reuse\n537\tinitially; later reuse needs generations checked at every internal handle lookup.\n538\t\n539\tExternal state inspection produces immutable named-field snapshots. Resuming one\n540\tsnapshot cannot mutate another snapshot's frames, values, or state fields.\n541\tPersistent snapshots share immutable arena objects, never a mutable guest\n542\texecution buffer. The first implementation may copy the active state shadow\n543\tat every public IVM transition; optimize only after snapshot-alias tests pass.\n544\t\n545\t### E.3 Wasm stream implementation reference\n546\t\n547\tThe adapter surface, import signatures, outcome encoding, canonical Jing bytes\n548\tand buffer discipline live in [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) §§1–3.\n549\tOwner decomposition, 2026-10-19: the former §E.3 content moved there unchanged;\n550\t§E.4 and §E.5 retain their numbers. Boundary doctrine and supersessions live in\n551\t[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md).\n552\t\n553\t### E.4 Composition adapters and the single in-instance engine\n554\t\n555\t#### E.4.1 Execution and embedding responsibilities\n556\t\n557\tNode/browser, Wasmtime and Wasmer provide the same stream implementation ([dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md)).\n558\tThe composition hosts the imported adapters, their underlying stream\n559\timplementations and bridge interpreters;\n560\tit does not implement resolve-var, primitives, stores, GC, continuations or the\n561\tscheduler for wasm. A WASI-based composition may supply stream implementations through these\n562\tadapters; the VM core imports no WASI. There is no JS dependency in native runs.\n563\tThe CLJS engine is the reference oracle, not a second production wasm engine.\n564\t\n565\tThe in-instance engine owns run-loop/scheduler-round, step budgets, ready/wait\n566\tqueues and terminal checks. A step performs exactly one guest instruction; run\n567\tkeeps the original ready-queue/terminal ordering and returns on the same blocked,\n568\tparked or halted states. Blocking is a normal result. Inspection/test snapshots\n569\tare immutable data, not an external engine service contract. Runtime start/drive\n570\texports may only enter the in-instance driver; they are lifecycle controls, not\n571\tapplication callbacks. No wasm stack survives a wait.\n572\t\n573\t#### E.4.2 Single-engine-port rule and Rust reconciliation\n574\t\n575\tThe production engine **is** the Rust design's `yin-value`, `yin-scalar`,\n576\t`yin-image`, `yin-kernel`, `yin-prims`, `yin-engine`, `yin-wire` workspace\n577\t(`yin.vm.rust.md` §9), compiled to\n578\t`wasm32-unknown-unknown` and linked with compiled register bodies. The internal\n579\thandle facade adapts values, never reimplements semantics. Native wrappers\n580\timplement only stream adapters and runtime mechanics. No parallel engine port\n581\tis permitted to bypass a crate's missing wasm support.\n582\t\n583\t“One transition implementation, two dispatchers” applies to the Rust interpreter,\n584\tcompiled wasm bodies and future LLVM helpers (`yin.vm.rust.md` §7.4 and §12 risk 2).\n585\tGenerated control flow uses shared transition helpers for call/return/restore/\n586\teffects; no wrapper duplicates them. CLJS necessarily has a language-port\n587\timplementation: it remains the shared-kit oracle, not literally the same binary.\n588\tCheck-then-commit, refused-step equality and per-machine fault containment remain\n589\tmandatory (`yin.vm.rust.md` §§5.3–5.5). Panics are kernel defects,"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_PNarCkmZm1e9npFlemCD6Qm6","content":"650\tthe lanes agree with each other and with the golden vector and address\n651\tfor every corpus program, and malformed input is refused by the\n652\tvalidator on the direct vector (one refusal row per rule). Equivalence\n653\tof a vector→datoms projection is retired, not deferred.\n654\t\n655\t## 4. Safepoints and the continuation format\n656\t\n657\t### 4.1 The safepoint table (UCF §7.4.1, rewritten)\n658\t\n659\t**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc).** The machine's task state between any two instructions, `⟨seg, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` beside its ordered waits and completed wakes, is canonical and lifts (continuation design §1.3): preemptive migration at any pc is a primary invariant of `yin.vm` (owner, 2026-10-17). A **safepoint** is a boundary at which the program stops by itself: a park with a pending wait, an explicit park with none, or a halt with a result. The table below enumerates those parking transitions; it is not the set of liftable pcs, which is every pc of every body. The semantic kinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\n660\t\n661\tEvery wait is one of the two closed states of §2.4 (`act` with\n662\t`{:deliver :rd}` or `tail` with `{:deliver :return}`); an effectful\n663\ttail call raises the `tail` form of whichever row its effect falls in.\n664\t`saved` is `saved(pc+1, rd)` of §2.4.\n665\t\n666\t| Safepoint kind | Raised by | State | Delivery |\n667\t|---|---|---|---|\n668\t| blocked read | `:stream-next rd c` → `blocked` | `act`, pending cursor cell `c` | `rd` ← read value; nil on `end`; `:dao.stream/gap` on gap |\n669\t| blocked write | `:stream-put rd s v` → `full` | `act`, pending retains `W[v]` and the stream | `rd` ← written value on retry `ok` |\n670\t| FFI call, sent | `:ffi-call rd op args` → `ok` | `act`, pending `:ffi` (call id, response cursor cell); in-VM `:reason :next` with `:call-id` | `rd` ← correlated `ok` value; `error` raises; bookkeeping removed |\n671\t| FFI call, retained | `:ffi-call` → `full` | `act`, pending `:ffi-request` (envelope verbatim); in-VM `:reason :put` with `:request-sent` | none at this phase; on append `ok` the entry becomes the sent phase |\n672\t| effectful call, non-tail | `:call rd f args false` whose operator yields a blocking effect | `act`, pending per the effect kind | per the kind's row |\n673\t| effectful call, tail | `:call rd f args true` likewise | `tail`, pending per the effect kind | **through return** against `K` |\n674\t| link request | `:module/require` miss, request in hand | `act` or `tail`, pending `:link-request` | none at this phase; on append `ok` the entry becomes link response |\n675\t| link response | request appended; polling for the correlated response | same state, pending `:link-response` | none at this phase; a matching `ok` makes it install; refusal raises |\n676\t| install | the child runs | same state, pending `:install` + the body's install entry | on `linked`: the module symbol to `rd` or through return; on `refused`: raises |\n677\t| halt | `:halt r`, or `:return r` with empty `K` | — | not resumable; a `:yin.k/result` travels |\n678\t\n679\tTwo cases sit outside the table: an **explicit park** (`:park rd`) is a\n680\t**no-wait safepoint**: it is a migration safepoint like every row\n681\tabove, but it raises no wait entry; its `act` record is the body's\n682\tparked record and a body of kind `:parked` names it by id (UCF §7.4.3\n683\tr5, \"explicit park is the no-wait shape\"). A **reified continuation**\n684\t(`:current-continuation`) is the one thing here that is *not* a\n685\tsafepoint: it is a captured value, never a task state.\n686\tA non-empty ready queue is carried as ordered completed wakes (continuation design §2.3); the holds (observation in flight, pending close) remain refusals of the lift as driver seams mid-step.\n687\t\n688\tThe \"effectful call, tail\" row is the one place the rewrite is more\n689\tthan notation: in the stack machine a tail effect's resume value landed\n690\tin `val` and the body's `:return` then delivered it; here the body has\n691\tno activation to resume into, so §2.4's `tail` state routes delivery\n692\tthrough `K`. Write-result and through-return delivery are distinguished\n693\tby the closed `deliver` record.\n694\t\n695\t### 4.2 The UCF frame\n696\t\n697\t`:yin.k/frame` (UCF §7.4.1) becomes, for the semantic profile:"}
